 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Heber died 1826",
  "tune": "public domain: Dykes died 1876",
  "arrangement": "Dykes's tune and bass (public domain), with the alto and tenor revoiced so the duet and trio are complete, and his bass changed in three places for beginners: on \"Lord\" and on \"mer-ci-\" the bass stays on the upper A where he sings the low A and leaps the octave, and on \"Ear-ly\" it sings B, the chord's root, where he has D sharp (the 1861 page, n199, read again in the quality audit, 2026-11-13); breaths written in for beginners. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): where the tune sits on its low E for \"Ho-\" \"ly,\" (the first and third lines) and on the last \"i-\" \"ty!\", the alto sings G sharp, a third above it, so the duet keeps the chord's third and the last chord is complete; the alto's rise of a fourth from the B7's D sharp (\"Ho-\") to the E chord's G sharp (\"ly!\") goes on up a semitone to A on \"Lord\" and on \"mer-\", and its rise of a fourth from A sharp on \"rise\" to D sharp on \"to\" holds there for \"Thee:\", each a move from one chord's third to the next chord's third; and on \"ing\" (\"morn-ing\") the leading tone, D sharp, is doubled by Dykes's own tune and bass (B/D sharp, as the 1861 scan prints it), the alto and tenor taking F sharp and B.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "Hymns Ancient and Modern, with accompanying tunes, ed. W. H. Monk (Novello, London, 1861), Hymn 135, in E, printed in minims (4/2) and written here in crotchets (4/4); the Amen left out; read from the scan at archive.org/details/hymnsancientmode00unse/page/n199"
 },
